%I A399348 #22 Oct 05 2026 19:06:02
%S A399348 1,2,3,11,13,23,29,79,199
%N A399348 Numbers k such that the number of consecutive values m = 0, 1, 2, ... for which |3*m^2 + 3*m - k| is prime is larger than for every smaller k.
%C A399348 The corresponding run lengths are 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 14, 23.
%C A399348 Since 3*n^2 + 3*n - k takes the same value at n and -1-n, a run of length L at n = 0..L-1 is a run of 2*L consecutive integer arguments n = -L..L-1 (with each value taken twice). For a(9) = 199 this gives |3*n^2 - 135*n + 1319| prime for n = 0..45 (23 distinct primes).
%C A399348 4*(3*n^2 + 3*n - k) = 3*(2*n+1)^2 - (12*k + 3), so the discriminant of the polynomial is 9 + 12*k. For every term except 1, 2 and 11, the run covers all n with 2*n + 3 <= sqrt(9 + 12*k).
%C A399348 No further terms for k <= 10^8. For 199 < k <= 10^8 the longest run has length 11 (k = 5377997).
%C A399348 The analogous records for 3*n^2 + 3*n + k, k > 0, are k = 1, 2, 5, 11, 23 with run lengths 0, 1, 4, 10, 22 (no further record for k <= 2*10^5); 3*n^2 + 3*n + 23 is the polynomial of A007637.
%H A399348 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Prime-GeneratingPolynomial.html">Prime-Generating Polynomial</a>.
%e A399348 a(9) = 199: |3*n^2 + 3*n - 199| for n = 0..22 gives 199, 193, 181, 163, 139, 109, 73, 31, 17, 71, 131, 197, 269, 347, 431, 521, 617, 719, 827, 941, 1061, 1187, 1319, all prime, while n = 23 gives 1457 = 31*47. No k < 199 gives a run longer than 14 (a(8) = 79).
%o A399348 (Python)
%o A399348 from sympy import isprime
%o A399348 def run(k):
%o A399348     n = 0
%o A399348     while isprime(abs(3*n*n + 3*n - k)): n += 1
%o A399348     return n
%o A399348 def aupto(kmax):
%o A399348     best, out = -1, []
%o A399348     for k in range(1, kmax+1):
%o A399348         r = run(k)
%o A399348         if r > best: best, out = r, out + [k]
%o A399348     return out
%o A399348 print(aupto(1000))
%Y A399348 Cf. A003215, A005846, A007637, A014556.
%K A399348 nonn,more,new
